   Applicat Inst; Joint Res Ctr European commiss; Tromso Satellite Stn AS;
   Space Imaging EOSAT; Norwegian Minist Environm},
Abstract = {Advances in active microwave sensors and processing have resulted in a
   new generation of affordable radar data. These developments include the
   capability to quickly generate detailed digital elevation models (DEMs)
   using airborne radar interferometry. The detailed DEMs created from
   Intermap's STAR-3i system have sample spacings as little as 5 meters
   with vertical elevation accuracy to 1.5 meters RMS. These DEMs are rich
   in information content, displaying both regional and sub-resolution
   features not apparent on the accompanying image data. The Familiar
   advantage of radar remote sensing applies; data can be acquired despite
   cloud cover or poor illumination conditions.
   DEMs are an excellent complementary or stand-alone data source,
   providing highly detailed information useful for numerous environmental,
   planning, resource, and military applications. DEMs are used to rectify
   any sensor data, to remove vertical height distortion and geocorrect the
   image. The resulting ortho-rectified images can be used as input into a
   GIS or as the source for thematic: or planimetric information
   extraction, Besides providing the typical DEM products (wire-mesh
   diagrams, perspective views, contour maps), the DEMs can be used
   quantitatively for such diverse applications as floodplain or watershed
   analysis, urban planning, and agri-business. in this paper, we summarize
   the STAR-3i system and provide examples of these applications.},
