Note = {1st International Conference on Sustainable Urbanization (ICSU), Hong
   Kong, PEOPLES R CHINA, DEC 15-17, 2010},
Organization = {Hong Kong Polytechn Univ, Fac Construct \& Land Use; Inst Urban
   Environm; Road King Infrastructure Ltd; Sun Hung Kai Properties; Chun Wo
   Dev Holdings Ltd; K C Wong Educ Fdn; Environm \& Conservat Fund; Gammon;
   Paul Y; Architecture Design \& Res Grp Ltd (AD+RG); China State
   Construct (CSCEC); Sino Grp; Vantage Int (Holdings) Ltd; ASCE Hong Kong
   Sect; ASHRAE Hong Kong Chapter; CIBSE; Construct Ind Council (CIC);
   Chartered Inst Bldg (CIOB); Council Sustainable Dev; Emerald; Hong Kong
   Inst Architects; Hong Kong Institut Engineers; Hong Kong Inst Surveyors;
   HKSTS; IUE; JSCE; RICS; Urban Planning Soc China; China Civil Engn Soc},
Abstract = {Is it a coincidence that seclusion of women from the preying eyes of
   males, in the Islamic traditional houses led to inward architecture?
   This is marked by architecture of the veil, similar to the standard
   Muslim woman's public attire (mode of dressing). Muslim men wearing
   turbans typified Islamic desired or highly valued practice (sunna).
   Courtyard house form meets social needs for privacy through hierarchy of
   spaces, and at the same time enhances thermal comfort via cross
   ventilation. All these and many more outline the accord between climate
   and culture in the traditional Arab house in the Middle East. Throughout
   the region various forms of traditional architecture combine distinctive
   decorative features with forms which have developed in response to the
   region's demanding climate. However, Arab architectural forms have to
   some extent been limited by three overriding factors - climate, culture
   and the availability of building materials. The chief feature of Arabian
   residential architecture has always been the measures taken to protect
   the inhabitants from the overpowering heat of the summer. This led to
   houses being built with thick walls for insulation, with few windows and
   with devices designed to take advantage of any potentially cooling
   breeze. There is paucity to implement environmental solutions in the
   design of new houses for the urban poor. Artificial ventilation seems to
   dominate recent house designs. Modern public buildings convey corporate
   image. Use of glass and modern building materials dominates the
   character of public buildings. This review paper first qualitatively
   identifies basic physical features of the traditional Arab houses in
   Tripoli, Libya. Subsequently, it measures how those elements adapt to
   the environment's culture and climate respectively. Finally, an
   assessment of any apparent harmony between identified house characters
   climatically and culturally could evolve. This paper contributes in
   establishing environmental congruence of man and his habitat. Social,
   physical and psychological dimensions of housing regionalism are
   symbiotic. In order to enhance housing sustainability, planners,
   designers and developers need to carefully harness these potentials
   economically.},
