   01-05, 2004},
Organization = {IEEE Engn Med \& Biol Soc; Whitaker Fdn; Cyberonics; NIH; NIBIB; NIDOCD;
   NINDS},
Abstract = {Under conditions in which the visual system can not reconcile dissimilar
   images from the two eyes, perception typically alternates between the
   two images - a process known as binocular rivalry. This paper presents a
   method based on the time-frequency analysis of the steady-state visual
   evoked potentials (SSVEP) that continuously registers the alternation in
   physiological dominance of the two eyes. Two images flickered at
   different frequencies as the visual stimulator to left and right eyes
   respectively so that the response can be recovered from the EEG by
   time-frequency analysis. The physiological dominance of each eye as
   evidenced by the SSVEP record correlates well with the subjects' report
   of perceptual dominance. This electrophysiological method appears to be
   suitable for measuring rivalry in non-verbal human or animals.},
