   Inst, New York, NY, NOV 30-DEC 01, 2000},
Organization = {Columbia Univ, HIV Ctr Clin \& Behav Studies; Netherlands Inst Social
   Sexol Res},
Abstract = {First-person reports of individuals' experiences of problematic ``sleep
   sex{''} were collected in an Internet-based study. Qualitative analysis
   of 121 reports yielded 6 distinct themes: (1) fear and a lack of
   emotional intimacy; (2) guilt and confusion; (3) a sense of repulsion
   and feelings of sexual abandonment; (4) shame, disappointment, and
   frustration; (5) annoyance and suspicion; ( 6) embarrassment and a sense
   of ``self-incrimination.{''} Results suggest that sleep sex can elicit
   negative emotions and cognitions that may become a source of personal
   and relational distress. Clinician familiarity with problematic sleep
   sex may foster more effective communication with individuals presenting
   with such complaints.},
